# Machine-readable citation metadata (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# GitHub reads this file to generate a citation for BacFlux. Zenodo does not: when
# a repository also has a .zenodo.json, as this one does, Zenodo takes the record's
# metadata from that file and ignores this one.
#
# AFTER EACH RELEASE, one thing has to be filled in here, because it cannot be
# known until Zenodo has archived the release: the VERSION DOI. Zenodo mints a
# new one per release under the concept DOI, and both are recorded in
# `identifiers` below - concept first, then the version. On the next release,
# REPLACE the version entry rather than adding a third: this file describes the
# version named above, not a changelog. The concept DOI never changes and is the
# one the README badge carries.
#
# `date-released` is the day the version below is tagged. It is set ahead of
# tagging; if the tag lands on a different day, correct it before tagging.
#
# Authorship: BacFlux v2.0.0 and later have a single author, listed below. The
# first Zenodo record, v1.1.5 (2024, doi:10.5281/zenodo.11143918), also credits
# Großkinsky D. K., Koch H., Trognitz F., Sanchez Mejia A. and Nagel M.; every
# later record lists the author alone. Work done with any release cites that
# release's version DOI. No ORCID is on file for the author, so none is given.
#
# The `references` list below covers the tools BacFlux depends on. It is a
# working subset: CITATIONS.md is the authoritative and complete list, and it
# carries the licensing notes as well. Where a reference has a long author list,
# only the first author is given and the entry says so in its `notes` field.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use BacFlux, please cite it using the metadata in this file — and also cite the tools whose output you actually used (see CITATIONS.md)."
title: "BacFlux: a unified Snakemake workflow for bacterial whole-genome sequencing"
abstract: >-
  BacFlux is an automated Snakemake workflow for bacterial whole-genome
  sequencing data. Version 2.0.0 unifies four previously separate workflows into
  one with four entry points — Illumina paired-end reads, Oxford Nanopore reads,
  hybrid Illumina + Nanopore, and pre-assembled contigs — converging on a single
  shared analysis spine: decontamination, assembly QC, completeness and
  contamination, taxonomic placement, functional annotation, secondary
  metabolites, CAZymes, antimicrobial resistance and virulence screening, plasmid
  and prophage detection, and an aggregated report. An optional mobilome module
  (off by default) reports, for each AMR gene, whether it sits in a mobile
  genetic element and how transferable that element is predicted to be. Two
  further optional layers use predicted protein structure: Baktfold names
  proteins Bakta leaves hypothetical, and a trait profile calls traits for
  plant-growth promotion, biocontrol, stress tolerance and bioremediation from
  sets of genes. Every tool runs in its own conda environment.
type: software
version: 2.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-28
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/iLivius/BacFlux"
url: "https://github.com/iLivius/BacFlux"

authors:
  - family-names: Antonielli
    given-names: Livio
    alias: iLivius

identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.11143917
    description: "Concept DOI — always resolves to the latest BacFlux release."
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.23736124
    description: "Version DOI — BacFlux v2.1.0."

keywords:
  - bacterial genomics
  - whole-genome sequencing
  - Snakemake
  - genome assembly
  - genome annotation
  - antimicrobial resistance
  - mobile genetic elements
  - insertion sequences
  - integrative and conjugative elements
  - plasmids
  - Illumina
  - Oxford Nanopore

